# --- util-linux subpackage over-attribution. Alpine splits util-linux
# into subpackages that all carry the *source* package's version, so Trivy
# reports the whole family's CVEs against whichever fragment an image
# happens to install — even when the vulnerable binary was never shipped.
# These four IDs are therefore scoped per image, on separate evidence, and
# granted to nothing else: the blocking, release and weekly workflow scans
# strip them from the baseline and hand each named image back only the
# subset its own review justified. An image that genuinely installs
# util-linux `mount`/`nsenter` still fails the build.
#
# postgres:18-alpine@sha256:d3e1620b... — all four. Trivy attributes them
# to the split `libuuid` runtime package. Reviewed 2026-09-08 against the
# exact deployed image: `apk info -e` reports `libuuid` only (not
# `util-linux`, `util-linux-misc`, `mount`, or `libmount`); `/bin/mount`,
# `/bin/umount`, and `/usr/bin/nsenter` resolve to BusyBox. PostgreSQL
# retains libuuid for UUID support, while the affected util-linux
# mount/nsenter implementations are absent. Re-check at expiry or remove
# sooner when Docker Official Images rebuilds with libuuid >= 2.42.3-r0.
#
# clamav/clamav-debian@sha256:4c975c43... — all four, and unlike the two
# images above this one genuinely installs util-linux (`util-linux`,
# `mount`, `login`, `bsdutils`, `libmount1` at 1:2.41-5, Debian 13.6), so
# the argument is reachability rather than absence. Debian marks all four
# `affected` with no patched package on any tier, so there is nothing to
# bump. Exploiting the mount/nsenter paths requires CAP_SYS_ADMIN, which
# Docker drops by default and which nothing in
# infra/docker-compose.single-vm.yml adds back (no `privileged`, no
# `cap_add` anywhere in the file); all four are local privilege escalation
# that presupposes code execution inside the container first. Re-check at
# expiry — this is the weakest of the three dispositions and should be the
# first to go when Debian ships a fix.
#
# redis:8-alpine@sha256:becdda6c... — 76642, 78408 and 78410 only; this
# image does not trip 78409, which stays blocking for it. Reviewed
# 2026-09-08 against the exact pinned image (Alpine 3.23.5): the only
# util-linux fragment installed is `setpriv-2.41.4-r0` (redis's entrypoint
# uses it to drop from root), and `apk info --who-owns` confirms
# `/bin/mount`, `/bin/umount` and `/usr/bin/nsenter` are all symlinks into
# `busybox-1.37.0-r30`. None of the three CVEs is in setpriv: 76642 and
# 78410 are util-linux `mount` (external mount helpers, X-mount bind
# options) and 78408 is util-linux `nsenter --join-cgroup`. Every alpine
# redis tag shares the 2026-08-20 rebuild, so there is no published image
# to bump to; remove this scoping once one ships setpriv >= 2.41.6-r0.
#
# ttli-api and ttli-web — all four, on the same reachability argument as
# clamav-debian. Both are Debian-based (api on trixie, web on bookworm via
# node:24-slim) and so ship the real thing: `dpkg -S` resolves
# `/usr/bin/mount` to the `mount` package and `/usr/bin/nsenter` to
# `util-linux`. Debian publishes no patched package on any tier, and
# apps/api/Dockerfile already runs `apt-get upgrade -y`, so there is
# nothing to upgrade into. Both images run as the unprivileged `app` user
# (uid 10001, never root) with no CAP_SYS_ADMIN, and all four CVEs are
# local privilege escalation that presupposes code execution inside the
# container first. Bump the base digests and re-scan at expiry; these two
# are the entries most likely to clear on a routine Debian rebuild.
#
# None of these is granted privileged mode or CAP_SYS_ADMIN anywhere in
# infra/docker-compose.single-vm.yml, which the mount/nsenter paths
# need — for postgres and redis that is a second line of defence behind
# the binaries simply being absent; for clamav-debian, ttli-api and
# ttli-web it is the argument.
CVE-2026-76642 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-78408 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-78409 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-78410 exp:2026-12-07

