CVE-2026-5773 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-6276 exp:2026-11-26
# The next three are vendored Go modules compiled directly into the
# caddy binary itself, not OS packages — worth a sharper look than the
# above precisely because caddy is the one component in this deployment
# with a host-published port (80/443, Caddyfile in
# infra/docker-compose.single-vm.yml). None is reachable through this
# deployment's actual Caddyfile (reverse_proxy + automatic HTTPS only):
# golang.org/x/crypto/ssh is a transitive dependency Caddy's default
# build never imports an SSH client/server from (no SSH-proxying module
# in this image); google.golang.org/grpc backs plugin-only features
# (e.g. a gRPC-based storage/cluster backend) this Caddyfile doesn't
# configure. golang.org/x/net/dns/dnsmessage and golang.org/x/text ARE
# on Caddy's real request/proxy path, but both CVEs need a malicious
# response Caddy itself requests (a DNS answer to Caddy's own upstream
# lookup, or text-processing of Caddy-originated data) — not something
# an inbound HTTP client controls directly.
CVE-2026-46600 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-56852 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-56854 exp:2026-11-26
CVE-2026-84304 exp:2026-11-26

# --- clamav/clamav-debian@sha256:4c975c43... two further Debian trixie
# findings, both `affected` with no patched package on any tier as of
# 2026-09-08, in an image this repo only pulls. Reviewed against the exact
# pinned image rather than the `stable` tag, which is mutable.
#
# CVE-2026-16742 is a systemd-homed local privilege escalation. Trivy
# attributes it to libsystemd0/libudev1 — the shared libraries — but
# systemd-homed is the vulnerable component, and it is not in this image:
# `/usr/lib/systemd/systemd-homed`, `/lib/systemd/systemd-homed` and
# `homectl` are all absent, and there is no homed-managed user for the bug
# to escalate through. Nothing but the libraries ships here.
CVE-2026-16742 exp:2026-12-07
# CVE-2026-86140 is a strcat stack-based buffer overflow in libxml2's
# `xmlSnprintfElements` (valid.c). This one is worth the sharpest look in
# the file, because clamd is the single component that parses
# attacker-controlled bytes by design — every learner upload reaches it
# (04 §3, REQ-BYPASS-08) — `libclamav.so.12` does link libxml2, and
# `ScanXMLDOCS` is on by default, so OOXML uploads really are parsed as
# XML. It is nevertheless unreachable: `xmlSnprintfElements` exists only
# to format DTD content-model validation errors, and libclamav never
# validates. Its dynamic symbol table references only the streaming
# xmlTextReader API — xmlReaderForMemory/ForFd/ForIO, xmlTextReaderRead,
# xmlTextReaderNext and friends — and contains no `xmlValidate*`, no
# `xmlParseDTD`, no `xmlSnprintfElements`, and no parser-property call
# that would turn validation on. xmlTextReader does not DTD-validate
# unless explicitly asked. Re-verify this symbol claim, not just the
# version, if libclamav is ever rebuilt against a newer libxml2.
CVE-2026-86140 exp:2026-12-07

# --- caddy:2-alpine@sha256:5f5c8640... five findings disclosed since the
