# no shell-out to curl, no cgo resolver — so nothing in this container
# drives libcurl with attacker-influenced input.
CVE-2026-11352 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-11586 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-8925 exp:2026-12-07
CVE-2026-9547 exp:2026-12-07
# GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf is google.golang.org/grpc vendored into the caddy
# binary — the same plugin-only surface as CVE-2026-56852 above. This
# deployment's Caddyfile configures reverse_proxy and automatic HTTPS
# only; no gRPC-based storage or cluster backend is built in or
# configured, so the module is present but never reached.
GHSA-hrxh-6v49-42gf exp:2026-12-07

# CVE-2026-84445 is another gRPC-Go xDS-server crash disclosed after the
# review above. Re-checked 2026-09-09: caddy:2-alpine still resolves to the
# pinned 2.11.4 digest, so no official replacement exists. The affected code
# is an xDS server handling malformed resource names; this stock image and
# infra/Caddyfile build/configure only Caddy's HTTP reverse proxy and automatic
# HTTPS, with no xDS server or gRPC storage/cluster plugin. Remove on the next
# official Caddy rebuild, or re-review at expiry.
#
# 2026-09-16 (T11): the same finding surfaced against the newly-added
# Prometheus and Grafana images (both vendor grpc-go too). Same argument,
# checked against each: Prometheus's grpc usage backs its OTLP receiver
# (disabled by default, never enabled here) and Grafana's backs its
# core-to-plugin IPC — neither is an xDS server, so both grants below are on
# separate evidence from Caddy's, not an assumption that Caddy's clears it
# for them. The CI workflow grants this exception to CADDY_IMAGE,
# PROMETHEUS_IMAGE and GRAFANA_IMAGE only; it remains blocking everywhere
# else.
CVE-2026-84445 exp:2026-12-08

# --- ttli-api: Debian 13 libexpat1 2.8.3-1~deb13u1. Trivy first reported
# these two advisories on 2026-09-09 and Debian publishes no fixed version.
# They require an application to parse crafted XML (hash-flood DoS and memory
# corruption respectively). TTLI's API source has no xml/ElementTree/expat/
# lxml/defusedxml import and no XML ingestion endpoint; uploaded SVG is rejected
# as an unsupported logo type rather than parsed. libexpat remains because it is
# a dependency of the CPython runtime, so it cannot safely be removed. The
# release workflow grants these entries to ttli-api:ci only; web, relay and all
# third-party images remain blocking. Remove as soon as Debian publishes a fix,
# or re-review source reachability at expiry.
CVE-2026-76956 exp:2026-12-08
CVE-2026-76957 exp:2026-12-08

# --- clamav/clamav-debian: Debian 13 libxml2 2.12.7+dfsg+really2.9.14-2.1+deb13u3.
# Trivy first reported CVE-2026-74860 on 2026-09-11 and Debian publishes no
# fixed version. The advisory is a double-free/use-after-free in libxml2's
# *Python bindings* (python3-libxml2). Verified against the pinned digest
# (`dpkg -l` inside the running container): the image installs no python3
# and no python3-libxml2 package at all -- clamd links libxml2.so.2 for its
# own XML parsing, but the vulnerable binding layer is absent, so the code
# path cannot be reached. ci.yml grants this entry to the clamav image only;
# every other image keeps it blocking. Remove as soon as Debian publishes a
# fix, or re-verify package absence at expiry.
CVE-2026-74860 exp:2026-12-11

# --- clamav/clamav-debian@sha256:4c975c43... two libpcre2-8-0 findings
# Trivy first reported on 2026-09-13. Debian has published a fix
# (10.46-1~deb13u2), but `clamav/clamav-debian:stable` has not rebuilt
# with it as of the same date (re-pulled and re-resolved the `stable` tag
# directly: it is still the exact digest already pinned here, still
# shipping 10.46-1~deb13u1) -- nothing to bump to.
#
# CVE-2026-86145 is an out-of-bounds write in `pcre2_dfa_match`, and its
# own advisory text says it "requires an attacker-controlled regular
# expression, or a recursive pattern in conjunction with a small heap
# limit" -- either way, control over the *pattern*, not the data being
# matched. CVE-2026-89161 is a use-after-free in `pcre2_jit_match`.
# Verified against the pinned digest by dynamic-symbol inspection
# (`objdump -T`), the same method already used for the libxml2 entry
# above: `libclamav.so.12.1.0` imports only `pcre2_compile_8` and
# `pcre2_match_8` from libpcre2 -- never `pcre2_dfa_match_8`,
# `pcre2_jit_compile_8`, or `pcre2_jit_match_8`. Swept every binary in the
# image that links libpcre2 (`clamd`, `clamscan`, `clamdscan`,
# `freshclam`, `libclamav.so`), and none imports either vulnerable
# function -- so both are unreachable regardless of where the regex
# pattern comes from. ci.yml grants both entries to the clamav image
# only; every other image keeps them blocking. Remove as soon as Debian's
# fix reaches the image, or re-verify the symbol table at expiry if
# libclamav is rebuilt against a newer libpcre2 (JIT could be compiled in
# then, even if unused today).
CVE-2026-86145 exp:2026-12-12
CVE-2026-89161 exp:2026-12-12

# --- clamav/clamav-debian@sha256:4c975c43... five more libxml2 findings,
# first reported 2026-09-16, all `affected` on Debian trixie with no
# patched package yet (still 2.12.7+dfsg+really2.9.14-2.1+deb13u3, same
# version as the 74860/86140 entries above). Checked with the same
# dynamic-symbol method used there: `objdump -T` against
# libclamav.so.12.1.0 extracted from the pinned digest. The full set of
# libxml2 symbols it imports is the streaming xmlTextReader API
# (xmlReaderForMemory/ForFd/ForIO/Walker, xmlTextReaderRead and its
# accessors), htmlGetMetaEncoding/htmlReadMemory, xmlFreeDoc,
# xmlInitParser, xmlGetDocCompressMode, and the xmlStr*/xmlFree
# primitives -- nothing else.
#
# CVE-2026-86139 (xmlURIEscapeStr, uri.c), CVE-2026-86142
# (xmlXPtrEvalXPtrPart/xmlXPtrEval, xpointer.c), CVE-2026-86143
# (xmlOutputWriteCallback/xmlBufUse, xmlIO.c -- the *serialization* path;
# clamd only ever reads XML, never writes it back out) and CVE-2026-86144
# (xmlXIncludeProcess/xmlXIncludeProcessTree, xinclude.c) are each a
# top-level libxml2 entry point absent from that import list, the same
# argument as 86140 above: unreachable regardless of what's inside a
# scanned document, because nothing in libclamav ever calls in.
CVE-2026-86139 exp:2026-12-15
CVE-2026-86142 exp:2026-12-15
CVE-2026-86143 exp:2026-12-15
CVE-2026-86144 exp:2026-12-15

# CVE-2026-86138 (xmlDictAddQString, dict.c) is a different shape of
# argument: dict.c is libxml2's internal string-interning table, called
# by the parser itself while tokenising, not a top-level entry point --
# it sits inside the xmlTextReader path above rather than beside it, so
# "not imported" doesn't apply. The integer overflow needs a single QName
# component (the prefix or local-name half of one element/attribute name)
# long enough to wrap a 32-bit size calculation in the arithmetic that
# sizes its heap allocation -- on the order of billions of characters in
# one tag/attribute name. clamd's own StreamMaxLength caps every file
# this deployment scans at 25 MiB by default
# (apps/api/src/services/antivirus.py's INSTREAM client relies on exactly
# that default, and nothing under infra/ overrides StreamMaxLength,
# MaxScanSize or MaxFileSize in clamd.conf). No single field inside a
# document can exceed the whole document's size, so nothing this
# deployment ever feeds clamd can get within many orders of magnitude of
# the length the overflow needs. Re-verify this bound first if
# StreamMaxLength is ever raised, not just at expiry.
CVE-2026-86138 exp:2026-12-15

