# --- grafana/grafana@sha256:ac461fb3... (T11 observability). Checking a
# specific version tag (12.3.1, the one first pulled) against `latest`
# turned up 48 findings, almost all `status: fixed` -- a strong "this tag
# is stale" signal seen before with Caddy. `latest` resolves to 13.2.2 and
# clears every OS-package and all-but-seven Go-module findings for free;
# pinned to that version tag rather than the mutable `latest` alias.
#
# github.com/grafana/tempo (2): both are server-side bugs in Tempo itself
# (an unbounded-memory query handler and an S3 SSE-C key disclosure via
# Tempo's own /status/config endpoint) -- this deployment runs no Tempo
# server and infra/grafana/provisioning/datasources/ configures only
# Prometheus, so Grafana only ever holds the Tempo datasource *client*
# code, never a reason to call it.
#
# go.opentelemetry.io/otel(/sdk) (2, CVE-2026-29181/39883 -- already
# granted above for Prometheus's own vendored copy on the same
# never-enabled-OTLP-receiver argument) and github.com/apache/thrift (1):
# Grafana's own tracing/Jaeger export, gated behind grafana.ini's
# [tracing.opentelemetry] section (confirmed via Grafana's own dependency
# history -- apache/thrift arrives transitively through
# github.com/uber/jaeger-client-go, Grafana's Jaeger tracing client, not
# any code path this deployment's minimal grafana.ini touches). This
# deployment's environment only sets GF_SECURITY_ADMIN_* and
# GF_SERVER_ROOT_URL; no [tracing.*] section exists anywhere in this repo.
#
# google.golang.org/grpc (1, CRITICAL, CVE-2026-33186 -- already granted
# above for Prometheus's own copy): Grafana's own use of grpc-go is its
# core-to-plugin-process IPC for datasource/panel plugins (a loopback
# transport between Grafana's own processes, not something an inbound
# HTTP client reaches). The specific CVE is in grpc-go's `authz` package --
# an opt-in HTTP/2-path-based authorization policy layer -- which nothing
# in Grafana's plugin transport configures; base grpc-go IPC does not pull
# authz in. Re-verify this one specifically at expiry -- it's the weakest
# argument in this batch, resting on how Grafana's plugin host uses
# grpc-go rather than a symbol-level check.
#
# golang.org/x/net/html (2, CVE-2026-25681/27136 -- already granted above
# for Prometheus's own copy): the one Grafana feature that plausibly calls
# this is the dashboard Text panel's HTML-sanitize path (sanitization is
# parse-then-Render by construction) -- reachable only by whoever can
# already edit a dashboard. This deployment's Grafana is never reachable
# through Caddy (T11 scope: "do not expose metrics publicly"); it binds to
# the host loopback interface only, the same pattern already established
# for Garage's S3 API above, so reaching the dashboard editor at all needs
# an SSH tunnel and a valid Grafana login first. Re-verify at expiry, or
# sooner if Grafana access is ever widened beyond loopback.
CVE-2026-21728 exp:2026-12-15
CVE-2026-28377 exp:2026-12-15
CVE-2026-43871 exp:2026-12-15

# --- grafana/loki@sha256:d70e4659... and grafana/alloy@sha256:b8ec653c...
# (T11 log shipping, added alongside the Prometheus/Grafana pair above).
# Both scanned clean at zero CRITICAL/HIGH findings on the pinned digests
# when first added on 2026-09-16. They still run through the same blocking
# `for img in ...` loop in ci.yml as every other image, on the base
# baseline, so a future finding on either fails the build like any
# unreviewed CVE would -- which is exactly what caught the entries below
# the same day, before this PR merged (a live Trivy vulnerability-DB
# update mid-review, not an image change on either unchanged digest).
#
# CVE-2026-84445 (the gRPC-Go xDS-server crash already disposed for
# Caddy/Prometheus/Grafana above, and granted to LOKI_IMAGE too as of
# this comment) started showing up against Loki's own vendored grpc-go
# (v1.82.1) between this pass's first scan and CI's own run -- a Trivy
# vulnerability-DB update, not an image change; the digest is unchanged.
# Loki's gRPC usage is entirely internal: distributor, ingester, ruler
# and query-scheduler communication between its own modules (visible in
# its own startup logs -- "ring=ingester", "ring=scheduler"). In this
# single-node deployment (infra/loki/local-config.yaml:
# replication_factor: 1, inmemory ring) there is nothing to actually
# connect to besides itself. None of that is an xDS server -- a
# Kubernetes/service-mesh integration this deployment has no use for and
# infra/loki/local-config.yaml doesn't configure.
#
# grafana/alloy@sha256:b8ec653c... picked up 3 findings the same way,
# same review pass. CVE-2026-84445 (grpc) is the identical xDS-server
# argument as above -- Alloy's own clustering feature
# ("starting cluster node" in its own startup logs) uses grpc for peer
# discovery, never an xDS server, and infra/alloy/config.alloy configures
# no clustering (single instance, no join/discover peers).
#
# CVE-2026-41567 and CVE-2026-42306 (github.com/docker/docker) are both
# specifically `docker cp` / archive-endpoint bugs: PUT and HEAD
# /containers/{id}/archive, exploited via a malicious container writing
# a trojanised decompression binary (41567) or racing a symlink into a
# bind-mount destination during copy (42306). Both need something to
# call the archive API in the first place. infra/alloy/config.alloy's
# discovery.docker and loki.source.docker components only ever call
# Docker's list/inspect/logs endpoints to find containers and tail their
# stdout -- there is no docker cp equivalent anywhere in this config, and
# nothing else in this image's own operation touches
# /containers/{id}/archive.
